+# Why the Approve button becomes one giant spinner
+
+## What the class combo actually does
+
+The bug is the bare `loading` class on the `<button>`:
+
+```jsx
+className={`btn btn-primary ${isPending ? "loading" : ""}`}
+```
+
+The teammate is writing this as if `loading` were a **button modifier** — the way it worked in DaisyUI v2/v3, where `.btn.loading` swapped the button's contents for a small inline spinner and kept the button's box intact.
+
+That modifier no longer exists. In **DaisyUI v4 and v5** (what Scaffold-ETH 2 ships today), `loading` is a **standalone component class**, not a button state. Its CSS is roughly:
+
+```css
+.loading {
+  pointer-events: none;
+  display: inline-block;
+  aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
+  width: 1.5rem;                 /* loading-md default */
+  background-color: currentColor;
+  -webkit-mask-size: 100%;
+  -webkit-mask-repeat: no-repeat;
+  -webkit-mask-position: center;
+  -webkit-mask-image: url("data:image/svg+xml,...spinner...");
+          mask-image: url("data:image/svg+xml,...spinner...");
+}
+```
+
+So `loading` turns **whatever element it's on** into a spinner by masking that element with a spinner-shaped SVG and painting the shape with `currentColor`. It is meant to live on its own `<span>`, e.g. `<span class="loading loading-spinner loading-sm" />`.
+
+## Why the label disappears and the layout jumps
+
+Put that class on the button itself and three things happen at once:
+
+1. **The text is masked out.** `mask-image` clips the button's entire rendering — background, border, *and* the `"Approving..."` text node — down to the spinner silhouette. The characters aren't hidden by `display:none`; they're literally cut away by the mask, so nothing readable survives. The mask paints with `currentColor`, giving you a solid single-color spinner glyph.
+
+2. **The spinner fills the whole button.** `mask-size: 100%` scales the spinner SVG to 100% of the element's box. On a normal small `<span>` that's a tidy ~1.5rem icon. On a full-width `btn` (SE-2 buttons are often wide, and full-width on mobile), the spinner is stretched across the entire button — hence "one big spinner filling the whole button," identically on desktop and mobile.
+
+3. **The layout jumps.** `.loading` forces `aspect-ratio: 1 / 1` and its own `width`, fighting the `.btn` sizing (min-height, padding, auto width from the label). The button's geometry snaps to the spinner's aspect ratio instead of its text width, so the box resizes when `isPending` flips — the visible "jump."
+
+Net effect: no label, a button-sized spinner, and shifting layout — reads as broken, exactly as QA reported. It's not a CSS-load or Tailwind-purge issue; the class is doing precisely what v4/v5 `loading` is designed to do, just on the wrong element.
+
+## The corrected button (idiomatic DaisyUI + SE-2)
+
+Keep `loading` on its own `<span>` **inside** the button, next to real text. `.btn` is already a flex container with a gap, so the spinner and label sit inline, centered, with correct spacing — no manual margins needed. Use `loading-spinner loading-sm` so the icon stays icon-sized regardless of button width.
+
+```jsx
+<button className="btn btn-primary" disabled={isPending}>
+  {isPending && <span className="loading loading-spinner loading-sm" />}
+  {isPending ? "Approving..." : "Approve"}
+</button>
+```
+
+Key points:
+
+- **`loading` moves off the button onto a child `<span>`** — the button box is untouched, so no mask clobbers the text and no layout jump.
+- **`loading-spinner`** picks the spinner style; **`loading-sm`** keeps it small and inline instead of scaling to the button.
+- **The `${isPending ? "loading" : ""}` template is gone** from the button class — that string was the whole bug.
+- **`disabled={isPending}`** stays; it blocks double-submits and gives the greyed/disabled affordance. (Drop `pointer-events` worries — `.loading` already sets `pointer-events: none`, but `disabled` is the correct, accessible guard.)
+- The spinner inherits `currentColor`, so it matches the button-primary text color automatically in both light and dark themes.
+
+This is the standard Scaffold-ETH 2 pattern for a pending tx button: a small inline spinner beside a visible, changing label.

